// RaceInFuture -- the paper's Example 3, races bounded in the future. The
// read of the fresh block happens before the block is published through g, so
// the other thread's store through g cannot reach it. Java and C++ allow
// ra = 7 by reordering the read after the publication; OCaml's causality axiom
// forbids it, which is why the model bans po from a read to a later write.
// sMRD allows it: the write of g does not depend on the read.
//
// The OCaml memory model (Dolan, Sivaramakrishnan & Madhavapeddy, PLDI 2018,
// section 6). An access in mode sc is to an atomic location, any other access
// non-atomic.
g := 0;
{
  rc := malloc 1;
  *rc := 42;
  ra := *rc;
  g := rc
} ||| {
  rg := g;
  if (rg != 0) {
    *rg := 7
  }
}
%%
forbid (ra = 7) [OCaml]
allow (ra = 7) [smrd]
